WEBVTT

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.315
Create a benchmark that represents the intended workload and can be repeated.

00:00:04.315 --> 00:00:08.570
Record the exact model, quantization, runtime, backend, settings, prompt,

00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:10.955
requested output length, and hardware state.

00:00:10.955 --> 00:00:13.415
Include one short interactive prompt, one normal task,

00:00:13.415 --> 00:00:16.680
and one stress case with longer context if the workflow needs it.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:21.040
Keep generation settings fixed so sampling variation does not obscure system changes.

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:24.270
Measure model load time, time to first token, prompt-processing rate,

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:27.127
generation tokens per second, and end-to-end completion.

00:00:27.127 --> 00:00:31.960
Track peak VRAM, system RAM, GPU and CPU utilization, disk activity, temperature,

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:34.267
clock speed, fan behavior, and power where available.

00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:36.825
A performance improvement that raises errors, throttling,

00:00:36.825 --> 00:00:39.548
or output defects must be evaluated as a tradeoff.

00:00:39.548 --> 00:00:40.620
Run several trials.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:43.355
The first run may include model loading and cold caches,

00:00:43.355 --> 00:00:45.800
while later runs may benefit from retained data.

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:49.459
Long sessions reveal thermal behavior that a ten-second benchmark misses.

00:00:49.459 --> 00:00:53.760
Report median or typical results and note outliers instead of selecting the best number.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:57.890
Test both an idle machine and the normal desktop environment if background applications

00:00:57.890 --> 00:00:59.465
are part of actual use.

00:00:59.465 --> 00:01:02.600
Change one variable at a time and log the result in a small table.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:05.975
Compare not only percentages but whether the difference is noticeable and repeatable.

00:01:05.975 --> 00:01:10.360
After synthetic tuning, return to real tasks and assess quality and usability.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:14.050
The purpose of a benchmark is to support a decision: keep the change, reject it,

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:15.880
or investigate a bottleneck.

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:17.880
It is not a score to maximize without context.
